          "replacement_value": {
            "id": "step-2-2",
            "claim": "Relations (5) and (6) are homogeneous with their actual label-dependent degrees. In far crossing commutation, both products have degree $-b_{\\nu_a\\nu_{a+1}}-b_{\\nu_b\\nu_{b+1}}$ because the disjoint crossings interchange the same two color pairs. A far dot move has degree $2d_{\\nu_b}-b_{\\nu_a\\nu_{a+1}}$ on both sides, as its untouched strand keeps the dot color. For adjacent unequal colors, dot sliding likewise preserves the degree of the crossing plus the transported dot. For equal colors $i$, the dot degree is $2d_i$ and the crossing degree is $-2d_i$, so both terms of $\\tau_ax_a-x_{a+1}\\tau_a=e(\\nu)$ and $\\tau_ax_{a+1}-x_a\\tau_a=-e(\\nu)$ have degree $0$, equal to the degree of the idempotent on the right. Thus every dot and far-crossing relation is homogeneous, without asserting that its common degree is always zero.",
            "step": "2.2",
            "inputs": [
              "step 1.1",
              "F1",
              "algebra"
            ]
          }
        }
