          "contract_raw_sha256": "b7378e8c036404e2b5d42a777223eb7310c9d80f8e11fb81997c142dd2c3112d",
          "json_pointer": "/contracts/lem-klr-maximal-tail-restriction-and-induction-have-a-unique-simple-head/derivations/0",
          "expected_value": {
            "id": "step-1-1",
            "claim": "(Nil-Hecke tail and its matrix units.) The constant-colour block $R(r\\alpha_i)=NH_r$ is the nil-Hecke algebra, and the explicit Artin polynomial/matrix-unit description exhibits it as a graded matrix algebra $\\mathrm{Mat}_{r!}(k)$ with its ",
            "step": "1.1",
            "inputs": [
              "F1"
            ]
          },
          "replacement_value": {
            "id": "step-1-1",
            "claim": "(Nil-Hecke tail and its matrix units.) The constant-colour block $NH_r=R(r\\alpha_i)$ acts on its polynomial column over $C_r=k[x_1,\\ldots,x_r]^{S_r}$. The Artin monomial basis and perfect symmetric-variable-linear trace of [[def-divided-power-klr-idempotents-and-projectives]] give integral matrix units and identify the full algebra with graded endomorphisms of that free rank-$r!$ column, hence with matrices over $C_r$ carrying the Artin degree shifts. Reducing the positive-degree central elementary symmetric functions to zero gives the graded matrix quotient over $k$; its column simple $L(i^r)$ is absolutely simple, and the divided idempotent cuts out a one-dimensional image. Its full-algebra polynomial-column graded projective cover has degree-zero endomorphism ring $k$, although its entire endomorphism ring is $C_r$. If $T$ is a finite-dimensional graded simple over $R(\\mu)\\otimes_kNH_r$, a positive-degree central operator has graded kernel and image. A nonzero such operator would therefore be invertible by graded simplicity, which is impossible on the finite nonzero degree support of $T$. Thus the positive tail center annihilates $T$, and it factors through $R(\\mu)\\otimes_k\\operatorname{Mat}_{r!}(k)$ with the indicated shifts. Matrix units identify $T$ with $N\\boxtimes L(i^r)$ for a graded simple $R(\\mu)$-module $N$, unique up to shift. This uses the augmentation matrix quotient for finite simples, not a false identification of the full nil-Hecke algebra with matrices over the ground field.",
            "step": "1.1",
            "inputs": [
              "given",
              "F1",
              "algebra"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
